# Logical and physical execution documentation review

Date: 2026-11-13

The ADR and public book now explain the same execution model: logical steps
exist before planning; planning selects an execution strategy; the backend
or its delegated engine schedules physical work. The meaning of `|` stays
stable across those choices. Alternative backends are explicitly design
direction, with `localhost` identified as the current implementation.

## Scope and reading path

- [Logical steps and physical plans](../public/learn/14-planning-and-backends.md)
  is a dedicated book page under chapter 14. It defines step, stage, task
  and job, compares execution strategies, and explains SQL translation with
  CTEs. Existing chapter numbers and page paths are preserved.
- [Chapter 14](../public/learn/14-etl-pipelines.md) introduces the distinction
  in its pipeline and effect sections. Chapters 6, 9, 13 and 17 and the book
  contents link readers to the dedicated explanation.
- [[HQL-0055]] states the preservation contract
  and delegated planning explicitly. Its four companion notes align the
  execution vocabulary, effects, local and scheduled execution, and the
  proposed completion type.
- [[HQL-0005]] retains the implemented
  annotation model, uses task terminology for scheduled work, and points
  to the draft extensions. HQL-0055 remains drafted.

## Review loop

### Pass 1: define the boundary

Made types, operations, dependencies and ordered effects part of the checked
logical program. Described physical planning as resolving execution choices
as far as the backend owns them. SQL generation is a translation handoff:
Spark still plans and schedules the submitted computation.

The book includes one small, runnable filter/map expression and a
hand-written SQL translation. Input positions and final SQL ordering make
the sequence-preservation requirement visible. These examples explain the
concept without introducing a production workload, backend API or promised
translation algorithm. The ADR companion's speculative distributed sink
plan and fixed worker counts were replaced with a description of planning
responsibilities.

### Pass 2: check consistency and guarantees

Corrected the following ambiguities:

- A logical step was called the unit of execution, obscuring physical stages.
- A pipeline file was called a job; a job now means one program run.
- The shell was called a backend; it connects processes that use HQL's backend.
- One SQL statement was equated with one task. Engine tasks and jobs now
  have an explicit distinction from HQL's units.
- Pure computation was described as freely reorderable. Dependencies,
  sequence order, results and failures constrain every rewrite.
- Partitioning a write appeared to weaken its delivery guarantees. The
  revised text requires the existing ordering and transaction contract.
- Local stage numbering and conceptual task vocabulary were presented as
  a global runtime model. The text now describes the actual per-pipeline
  annotations and the absence of local scheduler task identities.
- Completion of a sink was equated with completion of the whole job.
  The draft now allows other work in that job to remain.

### Pass 3: improve the reading flow

Replaced repeated negation-based explanations with direct descriptions of
what each term means and who chooses it. Kept the overview short enough to
introduce the topic and moved the detailed comparison into the dedicated
page. Added links where readers encounter SQL-like queries, endpoint I/O
and backend configuration. Distinguished current `Unit` behavior from the
draft `Step`/`Aute` proposal without changing runtime types.

## Verification

- `cargo test --locked --test tutorial`: passed, both tests. This runs the
  book's HQL examples, including the new expression and expected output.
- `.venv/bin/mkdocs build --strict --site-dir /tmp/hql-planning-docs-site`:
  passed after the final public documentation changes.
- Rendered HTML check: seven affected book pages, 1,183 local link and
  anchor references, all resolved; the new page appears in book navigation.
- `git diff --check`: passed.
- Engine terminology checked against the official
  [Databricks CTE documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-cte)
  and [Spark EXPLAIN documentation](https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-explain.html).

The SQL illustration was not executed against Spark or Databricks. It is
explicitly hand-written design material. Changes are in repository source;
the public site has not been deployed by this work.

## Decisions for a future backend

The next backend specification should define its supported expression
subset, handling of unsupported work, any partial pushdown or local
fallback, and the mapping between HQL runs and engine identities. It should
also establish conformance checks for ordering, missing values, numeric
behavior, diagnostics and effects against `localhost`.

Keep those implementation decisions separate from the book's stable
terminology. Add engine-specific plans and operational examples when an
implemented backend can generate and verify them. The logical graph IR,
adaptive planning details and partitioned write completion remain open;
the requirement to preserve the checked program's meaning is explicit now.
